howdy folks my name is Vic Iglesias I'm  a cloud solutions architect with Google  cloud and today I'd like to walk through  a architecture of a Kitab space CIC D  pipeline when we talk about CIC  pipelines we really have to think about  three different personas the developers  the operators and the security engineers  our developers are going to have their  own git repository where there's they  store their application code that  application code is also going to be  able to kick off continuous integration  whenever you get a commitment and that  continuous integration job is used to  create an artifact when we're talking  about deploying to kubernetes our  artifacts are container images so once  continuous integration creates that  container image it's going to store it  in a container registry that process is  going to go ahead and notify another  repository that something has changed in  that repository we have our operators  creating configuration that then says  how our application is actually going to  be deployed in order to manage that  configuration our kubernetes manifest  we're gonna use a tool called customize  customize is gonna add in that container  image reference into our config and then  commit that kubernetes set up kubernetes  manifests into a git repository that git  repository stores the state that we want  to apply into our environments the first  place where we saw that state is within  the staging branch of this repository  and that goes ahead and deploys it  straight to our staging environment now  developers can test out those changes  see them live in a staging environment  and once they're happy they're gonna  tell the operators that they want to  make a push to production or the  operators can decide to do that on their  own in order to do that they're gonna  merge from a staging branch of this  repository into the master branch and  that's gonna propagate those changes  down into the production environment now  we haven't yet talked about how we get  those production environments to be  secure the way we're going to do that is  by letting the security engineers have  their own repository that repository is  going to store the state that we're  going to use to apply policy across all  of our clusters we're going to use  anthos config management to do that  we can store that state in a repository  that has a structure that allows us to  apply to select clusters or all clusters  a certain set of configurations for  those security engineers those  configurations are going to be things  like  role-based access control policies  quotas for various applications and  things like restricting the ability of  containers to run as route so now they  can make all of those commits in an  audible get repo and then have them  apply to all of their production  infrastructure thank you for listening  please come visit us at the Google cloud  booth at cube con  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  you
 

